\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chapter 17: Corner Solution Responses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
17.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Motivation and Examples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now turn to models for limited dependent variables that have features
 of both continuous and discrete random variables.
 In particular, they are continuously distributed over a range of values,
 but they take on one or two focal points with positive probability.
 Such variables arise often in modeling individual, family, or firm behavior,
 and even when studyng outcomes at a more aggreagted level.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The most common case is when the nonnegative response variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, has (roughly) continuous distribution over strictly positive values, but
 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y=0)>0$
\end_inset

.
 We call such a variable a 
\series bold
corner solution response
\emph on
 
\series default
\emph default
or 
\series bold
corner solution outcome
\series default
, where the corner in this case is at zero.
 Corners can occur at other values, too.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Corner solution responses are often called 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

censored responses,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 a label that comes from situations with actual data censoring.
 Consequently, the leading model that we cover in this chapter is sometimes
 called a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

censored regression model.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Instead, we use the somewhat conventional name 
\series bold
corner solution model
\series default
 because we are trying to capture features of an observed corener solution
 response.
 The fact that observations of a dependent variable may pile up at certain
 focal points means that we might want to use special econometric models.
 But it is not a problem of data observability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In Chapter 19, we will study true data-censoring problems.
 There, we will be interested in an underlying response variable that we
 do not fully observe because it 
\emph on
is
\emph default
 censored above or below certain values.
 The econometric models for corner solution responses and censored data
 have similar statistical structures, but the ways one uses the estimates
 and thinks about violations of underlying assumptions are different.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the population, let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the corner solution response and let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the column vector of covariates (which contains unity as its first element).
 Assume
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=\max(0,x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is unobservable.
 Naturally, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 could have a variety of distributions, and its conditional distribution
 
\begin_inset Formula $D(u_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 could depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 But we will mostly work with the assumption
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u_{i}|x_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma^{2})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Assumptions (1) and (2) define the 
\series bold
type I Tobit model
\series default
.
 It is handy to have a notation for when a variable follows a Tobit model.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is determined by (1) and (2), we write 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{i}|x_{i})=\mbox{Tobit}(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta,\sigma^{2})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The normality assumption for 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 means that it has unbounded support, and, because 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, there is always positive probability (for any 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and any value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

) that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u<0$
\end_inset

, which means 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}=0|x_{i})>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Equation (1) has the benefit of directly relating the variable of interest,
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, to observed explanatory variables and an unobservable.
 Nevertheless, sometimes it is useful to write (1) as a latent variable
 model:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
y_{i}^{*}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i};\ u_{i}|x_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma^{2})\\
y_{i}=\max(0,y^{*})
\end{gather}

\end_inset

The latent variable formulation has the danger of suggesting that we are
 interested in 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}^{*}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

, but it will be valuable for certain derivations later on.
 Given (3) the latent variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the classical linear model assumptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
17.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Useful Expressions for Type I Tobit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Because 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a nonlinear function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

, we will want to derive various features of its conditional distribution,
 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Given that assumption (2) fully specifies 
\begin_inset Formula $D(u_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

, we can fully characterize 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 But before doing so, it is useful to derive general features of the conditional
 mean and median of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 that do not require full distributional assumptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|x_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Then,because the function 
\begin_inset Formula $g(z)\equiv\max(0,z)$
\end_inset

 is convex, it follows from the conditional Jensen's inequality that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{i}|x_{i})\geq\max(0,E(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}|x_{i}))=\max(0,x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore, although we cannot find 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 without further assumptions, we do have a lower bound as a function of
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, assume that rather than a zero mean, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Med}(u_{i}|x_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Unlike the expected value, the median operator pass through monotonic functions
, and the function 
\begin_inset Formula $g(z)$
\end_inset

 is monotonically increasing (though not strictly so).
 Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Med}(y_{i}|x_{i})=\max(0,\mbox{Med}(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}|x_{i}))=\max(0,x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and so, without any restrictions on 
\begin_inset Formula $D(u_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 other than a zero median (and certainly independence between 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not required), we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Med}(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 as a known function of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\beta$
\end_inset

.
 From equations (5) and (6), if 
\begin_inset Formula $D(u_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is symmetric about zero, it follows that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})\geq\mbox{Med}(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and has a normal distribution, we can find an explicit expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 We first derive 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}>0|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)$
\end_inset

, which are of interest in their own right.
 Then, we use the law of iterated expectations to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E(y_{i}|x_{i}) & = & P(y_{i}=0|x_{i})\cdot0+P(y_{i}>0|x_{i})\cdot E(y_{i}|x_{i}y_{i}>0)\nonumber \\
 & = & P(y_{i}>0|x_{i})\cdot E(y_{i}|x_{i}y_{i}>0)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Derive 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}>0|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is easy.
 Define the binary variable 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=1$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 follows a probit model:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P(w_{i}=1|x_{i}) & = & P(y_{i}^{*}>0|x_{i})\nonumber \\
 & = & P(u_{i}>-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta|x_{i})\nonumber \\
 & = & P\left(\frac{u_{i}}{\sigma}<\frac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\nonumber \\
P(w_{i}=1|x_{i}) & = & \Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

One implication of equation (8) is that 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\equiv\nicefrac{\beta}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

, but not 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 separately, can be consistently estimated from a probit of 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To derive 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i}y_{i}>0)$
\end_inset

, we need the following fact about the normal distribution: if 
\begin_inset Formula $z\sim N(0,1)$
\end_inset

, then, for any constant 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(z|z>c)=\frac{\phi(c)}{1-\Phi(c)}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is the standard normal density function.
 (This is easily shown by noting that the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $z>c$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\phi(\mathscr{Z})}{\left[1-\Phi(c)\right]}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{Z}>c$
\end_inset

, and then integrating 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{Z}\phi(\mathscr{Z})$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset

.) Therefore, if 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma^{2})$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{i}|u_{i}>c)=\sigma E\left(\left.\frac{u_{i}}{\sigma}\right|\frac{u_{i}}{\sigma}>\frac{c}{\sigma}\right)=\sigma\left[\frac{\phi(\nicefrac{c}{\sigma})}{1-\Phi(\nicefrac{c}{\sigma})}\right]
\]

\end_inset

We can use this equation to find 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 follows a Tobit model:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+E(u_{i}|u_{i}>-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\sigma\left[\frac{\phi(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})}{\Phi(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\Phi(\nicefrac{-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})=\Phi(\nicefrac{-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})$
\end_inset

.
 Although it is not obvious from looking at equation (9), the right-hand
 side is positive for any values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For any 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

, the quantity 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda(c)\equiv\nicefrac{\phi(c)}{\Phi(c)}$
\end_inset

is called the 
\series bold
inverse Mills ratio
\series default
.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)$
\end_inset

 is the sum of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 times the inverse Mills ratio evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is a continuous explanatory variable, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)}{\partial x_{ij}}=\beta_{j}+\beta_{j}\left[\frac{d\lambda}{dc}(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})\right]
\]

\end_inset

assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

 is not functionally related to other regressors.
 By differentiating 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda(c)=\nicefrac{\phi(c)}{\Phi(c)}$
\end_inset

, it can be shown that 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{d\lambda}{dc}=-\lambda(c)\left[c+\lambda(c)\right]$
\end_inset

, and therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)}{\partial x_{Ij}}=\beta_{j}\left\{ 1-\lambda(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})\left[\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}+\lambda(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})\right]\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This equation shows that the partial effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)$
\end_inset

 is not entirely determined by 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

; there is an adjustment factor multiplying 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

, the term in 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\cdot\}$
\end_inset

, that depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 through the index 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

.
 We can use the fact that if 
\begin_inset Formula $z\sim N(0,1)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(z|z>-c)=1-\lambda(c)\left[c+\lambda(c)\right]$
\end_inset

 for any 
\begin_inset Formula $c\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, which implies that the adjustment factor in (10), call it 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})=\left\{ 1-\lambda(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})\left[\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}+\lambda(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})\right]\right\} $
\end_inset

, is strictly between zero and one.
 Therefore, the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 is the same as the sign of the partial effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Other functional forms for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 are easily handled.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

All of the usual economic quantities such as elasticites can be computed.
 The elasticity of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}$
\end_inset

, conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}>0$
\end_inset

, is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)}{\partial x_{i1}}\cdot\frac{x_{i1}}{E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and equations (9) and (10) can be used to find the elasticity when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}$
\end_inset

 appears in levels form.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i1}$
\end_inset

 appears in logarithmic form, the elasticity is obtained simply as 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial\log E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)}{\partial\log(z_{i1})}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}$
\end_inset

 is a binary variable, the effect of interestis obtained as the difference
 between 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i1}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Other discrete variables can be handled similarly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can also compute 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 from equation (7).
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E(y_{i}|x_{i}) & = & P(y_{i}>0|x_{i})\cdot E(y_{i}|x_{i}y_{i}>0)\nonumber \\
 & = & \Phi(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})\left[x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\sigma\lambda(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})\right]=\Phi(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\sigma\phi(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

We can find the partial derivatives of 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 with respect to continuous 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 using the chaing rule.
 In examples where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 is some quantity chosen by individuals, this derivative accounts for the
 fact that some people who start at 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 may switch to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}>0$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 changes.
 Formally,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial E(y_{i}|x_{i})}{\partial x_{ij}} & = & \frac{\partial P(y_{i}>0|x_{i})}{\partial x_{ij}}\cdot E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)+P(y_{i}>0|x_{i})\cdot\frac{\partial E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)}{\partial x_{ij}}\\
\frac{\partial E(y_{i}|x_{i})}{\partial x_{ij}} & = & \Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\beta_{j}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The estimated scale factor for a given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}}{\hat{\sigma}}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 This scale factor has a very interesting interpretation: 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}}{\hat{\sigma}}\right)=\hat{P}(y_{i}>0|x_{i})$
\end_inset

; that is, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}}{\hat{\sigma}}\right)$
\end_inset

 is the estimated probability of observing a positive response given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}}{\hat{\sigma}}\right)$
\end_inset

 is close to one, then it is unlikely we observe 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 for a given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, and the adjustment factor becomes unimportant.
 We can evaluate 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}}{\hat{\sigma}}\right)$
\end_inset

 at interesting values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 to determine how the estimated partial effects change as the covariates
 change.
 One possibility is to plug in mean values, although this need not correspond
 to any particular population unit when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 contains discrete elements (and even sometimes when 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 contains all continuous elements).
 We can use median values, or plug in various quantiles.
 Often it is useful to have a single scale factor, and the scale factor
 that delivers APEs is probably the most useful.
 The APE for a continuous variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is estimated as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}}{\hat{\sigma}}\right)\right]\hat{\beta}_{j}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The scale factor in equation (15) is the average of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{P}(y_{i}>0|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 across the sample.
 The delta method can be used to obtain a valid asymptotic standard error
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $(15)$
\end_inset

but the bootstrap is convenient and feasible because estimation of Tobit
 models can be done fairly quickly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Naturally, the scale factor in (15) is always between zero and one, and
 that fact helps explain why Tobit coefficients are typically larger than
 OLS coefficients from a linear regression.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\gamma}_{j}$
\end_inset

 is the OLS estimate on a continuous variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 from the regression 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, we can compare 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\gamma}_{j}$
\end_inset

 to (15) as an indications of whether the linear model givens similare estimates
 of the APEs.
 Sometimes it will, but in other cases the linear model APEs can be notably
 different from the Tobit APEs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For discrete explanatory variables or for large changes in continuous ones,
 we can compute the difference in 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 at different values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 For example, suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

 is a binary variable, and define, for each observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, the two indices 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}_{i1}=x_{i(K)}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{(K)}+\hat{\beta}_{K}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}_{i0}=x_{i(K)}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{(K)}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i(K)}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $(K-1)\times1$
\end_inset

 column vector with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

 dropped.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}_{i1}$
\end_inset

 is the estimated index for person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}_{i0}$
\end_inset

 is the estimated index for person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Then the average difference
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\left\{ \left[\Phi\left(\nicefrac{\hat{w}_{i1}}{\hat{\sigma}}\right)\hat{w}_{i1}+\hat{\sigma}\phi\left(\nicefrac{\hat{w}_{i1}}{\hat{\sigma}}\right)\right]-\left[\Phi\left(\nicefrac{\hat{w}_{i0}}{\hat{\sigma}}\right)\hat{w}_{i0}+\hat{\sigma}\phi\left(\nicefrac{\hat{w}_{i0}}{\hat{\sigma}}\right)\right]\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is the estimated APE of the vinary variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}$
\end_inset

.
 Again, bootstrapping is a convenient method of computing standard errors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The equations for the partial effects in the type I Tobit model, equations
 (10) and (14), and the estimates (15) and (16), reveal an important point
 about the parameters: 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 as well as 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 appears in the partial effects of the covariates on 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i}>0)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 In a linear model, the variance of the errors plays no role in obtaining
 partial effects on the mean, and so the estimated error variance plays
 a role only in obtianing the usual OLS standard errors.
 But the variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 in equation (3) directly enters the conditional means.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Equations (8), (10), and (12) show that, for continuous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ih}$
\end_inset

, the 
\emph on
relative
\emph default
 partial effects on 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}>0|x_{i})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 are all equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\beta_{j}}{\beta_{h}}$
\end_inset

 (assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{h}\neq0$
\end_inset

).
 This feature can be a limitation of the Tobit model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By taking the log of equation (7) and differentiating, we see that the elasticit
y (or semielasticity) of 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 with respect to any 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is simply the sum of the elasticities (or semielasticities) of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)$
\end_inset

, each with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
17.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Estimation and Inference with the Type I Tobit Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ (x_{i},y_{i}):i=1,\ldots,N\right\} $
\end_inset

 be a random sample following the censored Tobit model.
 To use maximum likelihood, we need to derive the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 We have already shown that 
\begin_inset Formula $f(0|x_{i})=P(y_{i}=0|x_{i})=1-\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Further, for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{Y}>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i}\leq\mathscr{Y}|x_{i})=P(y_{i}^{*}\leq\mathscr{Y}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

, which implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(\mathscr{Y}|x_{i})=f^{*}(\mathscr{Y}|x_{i}),\ \forall\ \mathscr{Y}>0
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{*}(\cdot|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 denotes the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 (We use 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{Y}$
\end_inset

 as the dummy argument in the density.) by assumption, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}|x_{i}\sim N(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta,\sigma^{2})$
\end_inset

, so
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f^{*}(\mathscr{Y}|x_{i})=\frac{1}{\sigma}\phi\left[\nicefrac{(\mathscr{Y}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)}{\sigma}\right],\ -\infty<\mathscr{Y}<\infty
\]

\end_inset

We can write the density for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 compactly using the indicator function 
\begin_inset Formula $1[\cdot]$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f(\mathscr{Y}|x_{i})=\left\{ 1-\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\right\} ^{1\left[\mathscr{Y}=0\right]}\left\{ (\nicefrac{1}{\sigma})\phi\left[\nicefrac{(\mathscr{Y}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)}{\sigma}\right]\right\} ^{1\left[\mathscr{Y}>0\right]}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the density is zero for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{Y}<0$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\equiv\left(\beta,\sigma^{2}\right)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

denote the 
\begin_inset Formula $(K+1)\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of parameters.
 The log likelihood is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
l_{i}(\theta)=1\left[y_{i}=0\right]\log\left[1-\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\right]+1\left[y_{i}>0\right]\left\{ \log\phi\left[\nicefrac{(y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)}{\sigma}\right]-\nicefrac{\log(\sigma^{2})}{2}\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Apart from a constant that does not affect maximization, equation (18) can
 be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
1\left[y_{i}=0\right]\log\left[1-\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\right]+-1\left[y_{i}>0\right]\left\{ \nicefrac{(y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}+\nicefrac{\log(\sigma^{2})}{2}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial l_{i}(\theta)}{\partial\beta} & = & -1\left[y_{i}=0\right]\phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\left(\frac{x_{i}^{\prime}}{\sigma}\right)\left[1-\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\right]+1\left[y_{i}>0\right]\frac{(y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)x_{i}^{\prime}}{\sigma^{2}}\\
\frac{\partial l_{i}(\theta)}{\partial\sigma^{2}} & = & 1\left[y_{i}=0\right]\phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\frac{\left(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta\right)}{\left\{ 2\sigma^{3}\left[1-\Phi\left(\nicefrac{x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\right]\right\} }+1\left[y_{i}>0\right]\left\{ \frac{(y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta)^{2}}{(2\sigma^{4})}-\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}\right\} 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

The second derivatives are complicated, but all we need is 
\begin_inset Formula $A(x_{i},\theta)\equiv-E\left[H_{i}(\theta)|x_{i}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 After tediaous calculations it can be shown that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A(x_{i},\theta)=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
ax_{i}x_{i}^{\prime} & b_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\\
b_{i}x_{i} & c_{i}
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
a_{i} & = & -\sigma^{-2}\left\{ x_{i}^{\prime}\gamma\phi_{i}-\left[\frac{\phi_{i}^{2}}{1-\Phi_{i}}\right]-\Phi_{i}\right\} \\
b_{i} & = & \frac{1}{2}\sigma^{-3}\left\{ \left(x_{i}^{\prime}\gamma\right)\phi_{i}+\phi_{i}-\left[\frac{\left(x_{i}^{\prime}\gamma\right)\phi_{i}^{2}}{1-\Phi_{i}}\right]\right\} \\
c_{i} & = & -\frac{1}{4}\sigma^{-4}\left\{ \left(x_{i}^{\prime}\gamma\right)^{3}\phi_{i}+\left(x_{i}^{\prime}\gamma\right)\phi_{i}-\left[\frac{\left(x_{i}^{\prime}\gamma\right)\phi_{i}^{2}}{1-\Phi_{i}}\right]-2\Phi_{i}\right\} 
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=\nicefrac{\beta}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 are evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\gamma$
\end_inset

.
 This matrix is used to obtian the estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\theta})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Testing is easily carried out in a standard MLE framework.
 Sincle exclusion restrictions are tested using asymptotic 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistics once 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{j}$
\end_inset

 and its asymptotic standard error have been obtained.
 Multiple exclusion restrictions are easily tested using the LR statistic,
 and some econometrics packages routinely compute the Wald statistic.
 If the unrestricted model has so many variables that computation becomes
 an issue, the LM statistic is an attractive alternative.
 The Wald statistic is the easiest to compute for testing nonlienar restrictions
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, just as in binary response analysis, because the unrestricted model is
 just standard Tobit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
17.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Reporting the Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As with any estimation of a parametric model, the parameter estimates 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}$
\end_inset

 and their standard errors should be reported.
 We saw that the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{j}$
\end_inset

 give the direction of the partial effects on the means, and the ratios
 are the relative partial effects for continuous variables.
 The value of the log likelihod should be included for testing purposes
 and also to allow comparisons with other nonested models.
 A goodness-of-fit statistic for the conditional mean 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 is often of interest, particularly for comparing with other models of 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 (including linear models).
 A simple measure is the squared correlation between the actual outcomes,
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, and fitted values, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

, obtained by evaluating equation (12) at 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the MLEs, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}$
\end_inset

.
 It is also important to report partial effects.
 For continuous variables we can use (15) and for binary variables we can
 use (16).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
17.5
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Specification Issues in Tobit Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
17.5.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Neglected Heterogeneity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose that we are initially interested in the model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=\max(0,x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\gamma q_{i}+u_{i});\ u_{i}|x_{i},q_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma^{2})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is an observed variable that is assumed to be independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and has a 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0,\tau^{2})$
\end_inset

 distribution.
 It follows immediately that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}-\max(0,x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+v_{i});\ v_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma^{2}+\gamma^{2}\tau^{2})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 follows a Tobit model, and Tobit of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 consistently estimates 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{2}\equiv\sigma^{2}+\gamma^{2}\tau^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What about estimating partial effects on 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},q_{i})$
\end_inset

? We are often interested in APEs, where say, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},q_{i})$
\end_inset

 is averaged over the population distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

, and then derivatives or differences with respect to elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 are obtained.
 When the heterogeneity is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, the APEs are obtained by finding 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Naturally, this conditional mean comes from the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Under the preceding assumptions, it is exactly this distribuiton that Tobit
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 estimates.
 In other words, we estimate the desired quantities, the APEs, by simply
 ignoring the heterogeneity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not normal, then these arguments do not carry over because 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 does not follow a Tobit model.
 But the flabor of the argument does.
 A more difficult issue arises when 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 are correlated, and we address that in the next subsection.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
17.5.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Endogenous Explanatory Variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we now allow one of the variables in the Tobit model to be endogenous.
 The first model we consider has a continuous endogenous explanatory variable:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{1i} & = & \max(0,z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\alpha_{1}y_{2i}+u_{i})\\
y_{2i} & = & z_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2}+v_{i}=z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{21}+z_{2i}^{\prime}\delta_{22}+v_{i}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 are zero-mean normally distributed, independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 are correlated, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is endogenous.
 For indentification, we need the usual rank condition 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{22}\neq0$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is assumed to have full rank, as always.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Naturally, we are interested in estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

, but we are also interested in estimating APEs, which depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}=\mbox{Var}(u_{i})$
\end_inset

 .
 The reasoning is just as for the probit model.
 Holding other factors fixed, the difference in 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 changes from 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}_{2i}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}_{2i}+1$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max[0,\bar{z}_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\alpha_{1}(\bar{y}_{2i}+1)+u_{i}]-\max[0,\bar{z}_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\alpha_{1}\bar{y}_{2i}+u_{i}]
\]

\end_inset

Averaging this expression across the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 gives differences in expectations that have the form (12), with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}=[\bar{z}_{1i},(\bar{y}_{2i}+1)]$
\end_inset

 in the first case, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}=(\bar{z}_{1i},\bar{y}_{2i})$
\end_inset

 in the second, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma=\sigma_{u}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Before estimating this model by MLE, a procedure that requires obtaining
 the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{1i},y_{2i})$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, it is convenient to have a two-step procedure that also delivers a simple
 test for the endogeneity of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

.
 Under bivariate normality of 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

, we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u_{i}=\theta v_{i}+e_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\nicefrac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(v_{i})=\sigma_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 with zero-mean normal distribution and variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Further, because 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $(z_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Now, plugging in equation (26) into equation (24) gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{1i}=\max(0,z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\alpha_{1}+\theta v_{i}+e_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}|z_{i},v_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{e}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Using our previous notation, we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D(y_{1i}|z_{i},y_{2i})=\mbox{Tobit}(z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\alpha_{1}y_{2i}+\theta(y_{2i}-z_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2}),\sigma_{e}^{2})=\mbox{Tobit}(z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\alpha_{1}y_{2i}+\theta v_{i},\sigma_{e}^{2})
\]

\end_inset

It follows that, if we knew 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

, we would just estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

 by type I Tobit.
 We do not observe 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 because it depends on the unknown vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 However, we can easily estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{2}$
\end_inset

 by OLS in a first stage.
 The procedure is as follows
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm*
(a) Estimate the reduced form of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 by OLS; this step gives 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\delta}_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Define the reduced-form OLS residuals as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}=y_{2i}-z^{\prime}\hat{\delta}_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Algorithm*
(b) Estimate a standard Tobit of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 This step gives conssitent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The usual 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistic on 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

 reported by Tobit provides a simple test of the null 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\theta=0$
\end_inset

, which says that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous.
 Further, under 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=u_{i}$
\end_inset

, and so normality of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 plays no role: as a 
\emph on
test
\emph default
 for endogeneity of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

, this approach is valid without any distribution assumptions on the reduced
 form of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\neq0$
\end_inset

, the second-stage Tobit standard errors and test statistics are not asymptotica
lly valid because 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\delta}_{2}$
\end_inset

 has been used in place of 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Formulas for correcting the asymptotic variances can be derived using the
 formulas for two-step M-estimators.
 Note that joint normality of 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 is not necessary for the two-step estimator to consistently estimate the
 parameters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can recover an estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}$
\end_inset

 from the estimates obtained from the two-step control function procedure.
 From equation (26), we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{u}^{2}=\theta^{2}\sigma_{v}^{2}+\sigma_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is obtained as the usual estimated error variance from the first-stage
 regression, whereas, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

 are obtained from the second-stage Tobit.
 Forming 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{u}^{2}=\hat{\theta}^{2}\hat{\sigma}_{v}^{2}+\hat{\sigma}_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

 gives us all the estimates we need to obtain the APEs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As with all control function procedures, we can easily allow more general
 functional forms in both the exogenous and endogenous variables (such as
 squares and interactions).
 In fact, we we replace 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}=(z_{1i},y_{2i})$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=g_{1}(z_{1i},y_{2i})$
\end_inset

, then the esitmation procedure is unchanged.
 Of course, we must believe that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 has the reduced form (25) with the error term having the properties already
 described.
 Some additional flexibility is gained by allowing 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|v_{i})$
\end_inset

 to be nonlinear.
 When doing this, one should be away that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 will likely not have a normal distribution.
 Pratcially we may be satisfied with a conditional normal distribution for
 
\begin_inset Formula $D(u_{i}|v_{i})$
\end_inset

, even if that implies nonnormality of 
\begin_inset Formula $D(u_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A full maximum likelihood approac avoids the two-step estimation problem.
 The joint distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{1i},y_{2i})$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is most easily found by using 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f(y_{1i},y_{2i}|z_{i})=f(y_{1i}|y_{2i},z_{i})\cdot f(y_{2i}|z_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

just as for the probit case.
 Then density 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{2i}|z_{i})$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $N(z_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2},\sigma_{v}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 We already know that 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{1i}|z_{i},y_{2i})=\mbox{Tobit}(x_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\theta(y_{2i}-z_{i}^{\prime}\delta_{2}),\sigma_{e}^{2})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2}=\sigma_{u}^{2}-\theta^{2}\sigma_{v}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Taking the log of equation (28), the log-likelihood function for 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is easily constructed as a funciton of the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $(\delta_{1},\alpha_{1},\delta_{2},\sigma_{u}^{2},\sigma_{v}^{2},\sigma_{uv})$
\end_inset

.
 The usual conditional maximum likelihood theory can be used for constructing
 standard errors and test statistics.
 When the structural equation is just identified, the two-step and MLE estimates
 are identical (although the two-step inference needs to be adjusted).
 Once the MLE has been obtained, we can easily test the null hypothesis
 of exogeneity of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 by using the 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistic for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What if 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is a binary variable? Taking equation (24) as the structural equation,
 we could add
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{2i}=1\left[z_{i}^{\prime}\pi_{2}+v_{i}>0\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i},v_{i})$
\end_inset

 has a zero-mean normal distribution and is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 is standard normal, as always.
 Equation (28) can be used to obtain the log-likelihood for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is probit, its density is easy to obtian 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{2i}|z_{i})=\Phi\left(z_{i}^{\prime}\pi_{2}\right)^{y_{2i}}\left[1-\Phi\left(z_{i}^{\prime}\pi_{2}\right)\right]^{1-y_{2i}}$
\end_inset

.
 The hard part is obtaining the conditional density 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{1i}|y_{2i}|z_{i})$
\end_inset

, which is done first for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=0$
\end_inset

 and then for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Unfortunately, as in the probit case (and nonlinear models generally),
 the simple strategy of replacing the binary variable 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

, with its fited valued from a probit (or logit, or linear probability model)
 does not work; it is another example of a forbidden regression.
 In particular, the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 does not follow a Tobit
\begin_inset Formula $\left(z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\theta\Phi\left(z_{i}^{\prime}\pi_{2}\right),\kappa_{1}^{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 model for any variance parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa_{1}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Generally, 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{1i}|z_{i})$
\end_inset

 is difficult to characterize, although we could properly use it along with
 a probit specificaiton for 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{2i}|z_{i})$
\end_inset

 to perform joint maximum likelihood estimation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 is itself a corner solution and follows a Tobie
\begin_inset Formula $\left(z_{i}^{\prime}\pi_{2},\kappa_{2}^{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 model, then the MLE can also be derived.
 But again, we should not replace 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 with the estimated expected value 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{2i}|z_{i})$
\end_inset

 obtained form a first-stage Tobit.
 Such a procedure does not produce consistent estimates and may be very
 badly biased.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Because properly accouting for endogenous explanatory variables that have
 nonormal conditional distributions in Tobit models is challenging, one
 still sees linear models used when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is a corner, and then standard IV methods, such as 2SLS, can be applied
 regardless of the nature of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

.
 As with binary responses, the linear model has been much maligned for corner
 solution responses, but a linear model estimated by 2SLS can deliver good
 estimates of avarage effects.
 One of the inappropriate two-step approaches described previously, where
 fitted probit or Tobit estimates are inserted into a second-stage Tobit,
 is likely inferior to a linear model that has been properly estimated by
 instrumental variables.
 An interesting topic is to find control function methods that can be used
 for general 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 that do not suffer from a borbidden regression problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
17.5.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Heteroskedasticity and Nonormality in the Latent Variable Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As in the case of probit, both heteroskedasticity and nonnormality result
 in the Tobit estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 being inconsistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 This inconsistency occurs because the derived density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 hinges crucially on 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}|x_{i}\sim N(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta,\sigma^{2})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Rather than focusing on parameters, we must remember that the presence of
 heteroskedasticity or nonnormality in the latent variable model entirely
 changes the functional forms for 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, it does not make sense to focus only on the inconsistency in
 estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 We should study how departures from the homoskedastic normal assumption
 affect the estimated partial derivatives of the conditional mean functions.
 Alllowing for heteroskedasticity or nonnormality in the latent variable
 model can be useful for generalizing functional form in corner solution
 applications, and it should be viewed in that light.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Specification tests can be based on the score approach, where the standard
 Tobit model is nested in a more general alternative.
 Test for heteroskedasticity and nonnormality in the latent variable equation
 are easily constructed if the outer product of the form statistic is used.
 A useful test for heteroskedasticity is obtianed by assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{i}|x_{i})=\sigma^{2}\exp(x_{1i}^{\prime}\delta)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\times1$
\end_inset

 subvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1i}$
\end_inset

 doe not include a constant).
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 restrictions 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\delta=0$
\end_inset

 can be tested using the LM statistic.
 The partial derivatives of the log likelihood 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}(\beta,\sigma^{2},\delta)$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

, evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta=0$
\end_inset

, are given exactly as in equations (19) and (20).
 Further, we can show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial l_{i}}{\partial\delta}=\sigma^{2}x_{1i}\left(\nicefrac{\partial l_{i}}{\partial\sigma^{2}}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus the outer product of the score statistic 
\begin_inset Formula $N-SSR_{0}$
\end_inset

 from the regression
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
1\ \mbox{on}\ \nicefrac{\partial\hat{l}_{i}}{\partial\beta},\nicefrac{\partial\hat{l}_{i}}{\partial\sigma^{2}},\hat{\sigma}^{2}x_{1i}\left(\nicefrac{\partial\hat{l}_{i}}{\partial\sigma^{2}}\right),\ i=1,\ldots,N
\]

\end_inset

where the derivatives are evaluated at the Tobit estimates (the restricted
 estimates) and 
\begin_inset Formula $SSR_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the usual sum of squared residuals.
 Under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N-SSR_{0}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{a}\\
\sim
\end{array}\chi_{Q}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Unfortunately, the outer product form of the statistic can reject much
 too often when the null hypothesis is true.
 If MLE of the alternative model is possible, the LR statistic is a preferable
 alternative.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can also construct tests of nonnormality that require only standard Tobit
 estimation.
 The most convenient of these are derived as conditional moment tests.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is not too difficult to estimate Tobit models with 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 heteroskedastic if a test reveals such a problem.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 are of interest, we should look at estimates of these expectations with
 and without heteroskedasticity.
 The partial effects on 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},y_{i}>0)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

 could be similar even though the estimates of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 might be very different.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As a rough idea of the appropriateness of the standard Tobit model, we can
 compare the probit esitmates, say 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\gamma}$
\end_inset

, to the Tobit estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=\nicefrac{\beta}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

, namely, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\hat{\beta}}{\hat{\sigma}}$
\end_inset

.
 These will never be identical, but the should not be statistically different.
 Statistically significant sign changes are indications of misspecification.
 For example, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\gamma}_{j}$
\end_inset

 is positive and significant but 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{j}$
\end_inset

 is negative and berhaps significant, the Tobit model is probably misspecified.
\end_layout

